            <ArticleTitle>Bipolar Depression and Cognitive Impairment: Shared Mechanisms and New Treatment Avenues.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>95-109</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.psc.2015.09.004</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0193-953X(15)00099-4</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Depression and cognitive impairment are pervasive and highly disabling aspects of bipolar disorder. Although cognitive impairment is partially independent from mood episodes, depressive symptoms may increase the risk of cognitive impairment in bipolar disorder through inflammatory processes as well as health risks such as obesity and sedentary behavior. Novel treatment avenues at the intersection of bipolar depression and cognitive impairment target inflammation directly or indirectly health behaviors such as diet, physical activity, and sleep hygiene.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Published by Elsevier Inc.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
